# Template for local API keys — copy this file to .env.local and fill in your
# own keys. .env.local is gitignored so real keys never reach the repository.
#
# The prefix is the whole security story here:
#
#   VITE_*     -> compiled into the browser bundle. PUBLIC. Anyone can read it.
#   no prefix  -> stays in the Node/Vite process. Never sent to the browser.
#
# ── MapTiler — public on purpose ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Maps and geocoding run in the browser, so this key has to be reachable from
# client code. That is safe ONLY because MapTiler keys can be locked to a list
# of allowed origins. In https://cloud.maptiler.com/account/keys/ add:
#     http://localhost:5173    (dev server)
#     http://localhost:5174    (Playwright e2e server)
#     https://your-domain.tld  (production)
# Without that list the key works from anyone's site and your quota is theirs.
# A restricted key answers 403 "Key usage restricted" from every other origin.
VITE_MAPTILER_KEY=

# ── Pexels — server-side only ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOTE THE MISSING VITE_ PREFIX. It is deliberate and load-bearing.
# Pexels offers no origin restriction, so a Pexels key in the bundle is simply
# a published credential. It is therefore only ever read by a server:
#   * dev:        the Vite middleware in vite.config.js reads PEXELS_API_KEY
#   * production: public/api/pexels.php reads a private file OUTSIDE public_html
#                 (see deploy/weathersphere-secrets.example.php)
# The browser only ever calls the same-origin endpoint /api/pexels.php.
# Leave this empty to fall back to the gradient/emoji visuals — nothing breaks.
# Get a free key at https://www.pexels.com/api/
PEXELS_API_KEY=
